# ROLES — {{PROJECT_NAME}} sub-agent roster

> The human-readable roster of every role on the team + the global/local capability
> split. This doc **indexes** the sub-agents; the *full* system prompt of each lives
> in `.claude/agents/<name>.md` (loaded by the runtime when dispatched, not pasted
> here). `doctor` checks that this roster and `.claude/agents/` agree. Loaded once
> per session (boot set).

## 👥 Sub-agent registry (local — built/forked for this project)
| subagent_type | role (one line) | step | invocation | mainly uses (skill pointers) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `{{name}}` | {{role}} | {{①}} | {{parallel-batch\|singleton}} | {{skill X, skill Y}} |
| `{{name}}` | {{role}} | {{②}} | {{…}} | {{…}} |
| `dev-maintainer` | dev & maintenance: ALL code / SP / skill / template changes | as needed | singleton | the local CLI; `self-reflection*` |

> **Invocation modes:** `parallel-batch` = fan out N instances at once (mark the seam in
> WORKFLOW.md); `singleton` = exists for role clarity, runs single-threaded;
> `external-bridge` = not dispatched natively — engaged via a partner protocol (see below).
> **Dispatch:** `Agent(subagent_type="<name>", prompt="project context + self-contained task brief")`.
> Send independent agents in **one message** to run them in parallel (Philosophy 2).
> **No skill-less roles:** every role names ≥1 skill it mainly uses; each skill declares
> the MCP/CLI it is built from. A role with no skill pointer is an employee with no tools.
> **No self-check, no report (Philosophy 10):** every task brief names the step's QA
> command; the sub-agent runs `{{core}} qa <step> <task_id>` before reporting and
> attaches the passing output. A completion report without it is invalid.
> **All outbound communication to the user funnels through the CEO** — sub-agents never message the user directly.
> **Anti-proliferation:** prefer one shared maintainer/role over one-per-artifact; split a role out only when an artifact has genuinely distinct dependencies.

## 🤝 External contract partners (engaged by contract, not managed by prompt)
> Agents *outside* this runtime that own a capability our staff lacks. The CEO cannot
> dispatch them natively: engagement = write a **contract file** (deliverables, absolute
> paths, format, naming) → wake the partner via its protocol → await its **completion
> report** → CEO reviews before accepting. A partner sees only what the contract states.

| partner | capabilities | invocation protocol | contract & report |
|---|---|---|---|
| `{{partner}}` | {{what it does for us}} | {{file bridge / API / queue}} → `playbooks/partner_protocol_{{partner}}.md` | {{contract path convention → report path convention}} |

> **Internal-first economics (Philosophy 2):** internal dispatches ride the runtime we
> already pay for; external LLM API calls burn extra credits. Never outsource what an
> internal sub-agent can do — a partner or external API is engaged only for a genuine
> **capability gap**, never for convenience or capacity.
> Delete this section if the project has no external partners — do not invent one.

## 🧰 Capability layers (each role narrows what it should use)
> A role can *see* a large pile of skills, but **seeing ≠ should-use**. Each SP must
> explicitly narrow ("your work mainly uses skill X / Y") so it doesn't grab tools at random.

